// def_order.jtr — a struct is emitted AFTER the typedefs its fields embed by value.
//
// C has no forward reference for a by-value member: `struct Holder { JestyrSlice_i32 xs; }`
// needs `JestyrSlice_i32` to be a COMPLETE type at that point, not merely declared. But the
// backend emits aggregates in phases — structs and enums first, then the synthesized slice,
// genref and array typedefs — so the natural emission order is exactly backwards for a
// struct that holds one of them.
//
// The fix is not to reshuffle the phases (their order is load-bearing elsewhere); it is that
// the whole aggregate window emits into a capture buffer whose segments are re-emitted in
// dependency order. Each definition registers the names it embeds BY VALUE, and a stable
// post-order DFS puts dependencies first. A program with no forward dependency comes out in
// the original order, so the reordering is invisible unless it is needed.
//
// The dependency rule is one line of C semantics: a rendered field type containing `*` needs
// only a forward declaration, so it contributes no edge. That is why `p` below creates no
// dependency at all, and why the three typedefs themselves depend on nothing — a slice is
// `{ E* ptr; len }` and a genref is `{ E* ptr; gen }`, both of which reach `E` through a
// pointer. Only the array typedef embeds its element by value, and so depends on it.
//
// Nothing here ever CONSTRUCTS a slice or a genref: the field declarations alone are what put
// the edges there. That is the case worth pinning, because a backend that discovered its
// typedef dependencies from values rather than declarations would pass every other test in
// the corpus and emit C that does not compile for this one.
struct Holder {
    xs: []i32,      // by value -> depends on JestyrSlice_i32
    r: &i64,        // by value -> depends on JestyrRef_i64
    a: [4]i64,      // by value -> depends on JestyrArr_i64_4
    p: *mut u8,     // a POINTER -> no dependency; a forward declaration suffices
}

fn main() -> i32 {
    // The array typedef is collected from VALUES rather than declarations, so `Holder.a`
    // alone would not bring `JestyrArr_i64_4` into existence. This literal is what does.
    let v: [4]i64 = [1, 2, 3, 4]
    print_int(v[0])

    // Asking C for the size proves the ordering held: `sizeof` on an incomplete member type
    // is not a warning, it is a compile error — so if the typedefs landed after the struct,
    // this file would not build at all.
    print_int(size_of(Holder) as i64)
    return 0
}
